@@s001 Code: mergesort in Java
@@s002 Code: mergesort in Java
@@s003 Divide and conquer in Java: recursion on indices, the merge with two fronts and copying the result back.
@@s004 Divide and recurse
@@s005 The function sorts the subinterval <code>a[lo..hi)</code>. It divides in half and calls itself on the two halves.
@@s006 Divide and recurse
@@s007 Base case: a single element or none is already sorted.
@@s008 The split point. With integers, division truncates: it is the centre rounded down.
@@s009 I sort the left half, then the right one. The recursion is the first part of divide and conquer.
@@s010 Syntax notes
@@s011 The intervals are half-open: <code>[lo, hi)</code> includes <code>lo</code> and excludes <code>hi</code>. It is Java's convention for indices.
@@s012 Merging
@@s013 Two fronts, one per half. At each step I copy the smaller into the temporary array.
@@s014 Merging
@@s015 I compare the two fronts and copy the smaller. <code>k++</code> uses the value and then advances.
@@s016 I copy back the merged part: <code>arraycopy</code> copies blocks of arrays.
@@s017 Syntax notes
@@s018 <code>i++</code> inside an expression uses the value before the increment: here it moves the front forward by one.
@@s019 Running it
@@s020 Example of input and output (actually compiled and run).
